<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Miltiades</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 550–489 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> · Also known as Miltiades the Younger</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Miltiades was an Athenian general who ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli">Thracian Chersonese</a> before fleeing to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> in 493 BCE. As one of the ten generals he persuaded the Athenians to fight and led them to victory at Marathon in 490 BCE. A failed attack on Paros the next year led to his trial and a heavy fine, and he died of a wound soon after; his son was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimon">Cimon</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/miltiades.jpg" alt="Miltiades" width="320" height="426" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Petar Milošević · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Miltiades_(Roman_replica).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
